     "text": [
      "{'channel': 'Google for Developers',\n",
      " 'description': \"Learn how EmbeddingGemma 2, Google's open, lightweight \"\n",
      "                'multimodal embedding model, maps text, code, images, video, '\n",
      "                'and audio into a single, unified embedding space. \\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                'The video covers:\\n'\n",
      "                '* How modality-specific encoders and a bidirectional '\n",
      "                'transformer turn multimodal data into a single '\n",
      "                'representation.\\n'\n",
      "                '* The two-stage training recipe: initializing from Gemma 4 '\n",
      "                'and distilling from Gemini Embedding 2, then finetuning with '\n",
      "                'contrastive and spread-out losses.\\n'\n",
      "                '* Efficiency techniques for running in resource-constrained '\n",
      "                'environments.\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                'Have questions? Drop them in the comments.\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                '🔗Resources:\\n'\n",
      "                'Read EmbeddingGemma 2 tech report → https://goo.gle/4eAIY7W \\n'\n",
      "                'Download model weights → https://goo.gle/4fbzYpU\\n'\n",
      "                'View docs → https://goo.gle/3WJLEKh\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                'Subscribe to Google for Developers → '\n",
      "                'https://goo.gle/developers    \\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                '\\n'\n",
      "                'Speakers: Henrique Schechter Vera, Sahil Dua\\n'\n",
      "                'Products Mentioned: Gemma',\n",
      " 'title': 'EmbeddingGemma 2: How natively multimodal embeddings work',\n",
      " 'transcript': \"Computers know how to find the photos you're looking for, \"\n",
      "               \"detect spam in your inbox, and recommend videos you'd like to \"\n",
      "               'watch, but how do they actually understand the meaning behind '\n",
      "               'your photos, emails, or videos? Often, the answer lies in '\n",
      "               'embeddings. An embedding is a list of numbers, a '\n",
      "               'high-dimensional vector that captures the meaning of a piece '\n",
      "               'of data. The closer two vectors are in space, the more '\n",
      "               'semantically similar the data they represent. - The new '\n",
      "               'EmbeddingGemma 2 model maps text, code, images, videos, and '\n",
      "               'audio into a unified embedding space. This means the '\n",
      "               'embeddings for a photo of a sunset and the text golden hour '\n",
      "               'end up right next to each other. You can even combine '\n",
      "               'modalities into one input. The model natively understands '\n",
      "               'interleaved sequences of different modalities to create a '\n",
      "               'single unified representation of the content. These embeddings '\n",
      "               'can be used to retrieve relevant documents for a given query, '\n",
      "               'classify them into different types, cluster them into groups, '\n",
      "               \"and much more. Let's break down how EmbeddingGemma 2 works. - \"\n",
      "               'Like the previous version of EmbeddingGemma, a single neural '\n",
      "               'network backbone creates the final embedding representations '\n",
      "               'of our data. With EmbeddingGemma 2, the difference is in how '\n",
      "               'inputs are processed before an embedding is generated. Instead '\n",
      "               'of feeding everything immediately into a single model, raw '\n",
      "               'inputs first go through modality specific encoders. The vision '\n",
      "               'encoder chops images or video frames into small patches. The '\n",
      "               'audio encoder converts raw sound waveforms into a visual '\n",
      "               'representation called a spectrogram and slices these '\n",
      "               'representations into short segments. Each encoder then outputs '\n",
      "               'vectors, one for each image patch and one for each 40 '\n",
      "               'millisecond sound interval, and projects them into a shared '\n",
      "               'space that serves as a common language for the backbone to '\n",
      "               'process all the modalities together. - At the core sits a '\n",
      "               'bidirectional transformer which weighs the relevance of '\n",
      "               'different parts of the input, contextualizes them, and blends '\n",
      "               'together information across different modalities. Then three '\n",
      "               'steps collapse the intermediate output from the transformer '\n",
      "               'into a single embedding. Mean pooling averages vectors, '\n",
      "               'compressing the sequence into a single representation. A dense '\n",
      "               'layer projects the pooled vector into its final output shape. '\n",
      "               'Finally, normalization maps the vector onto unit sphere for '\n",
      "               'both training stability and efficient comparisons. - One key '\n",
      "               'improvement in EmbeddingGemma 2 is per-layer inputs. Instead '\n",
      "               'of feeding the input context into the transformer just once, '\n",
      "               'we inject a tailored version of the input at every layer. With '\n",
      "               'this, we ensure the original signal is not lost even at its '\n",
      "               'deepest layers. Thanks to these and other upgrades, '\n",
      "               'EmbeddingGemma 2 is able to stay leaner and faster than its '\n",
      "               'predecessor. - So how is EmbeddingGemma 2 trained to generate '\n",
      "               'multimodal embeddings? The training regime is two stages, '\n",
      "               \"pre-finetuning and finetuning. First of all, we don't train \"\n",
      "               'from scratch. EmbeddingGemma 2 is initialized from a '\n",
      "               'pre-trained Gemma 4 decoder model, so it inherits '\n",
      "               'comprehensive world knowledge before the embedding training '\n",
      "               'even begins. Then it learns from the best. Through '\n",
      "               'distillation, EmbeddingGemma 2 studies the embedding space of '\n",
      "               'its much larger teacher, Gemini Embedding 2. - During '\n",
      "               \"finetuning, we refine EmbeddingGemma's representations by \"\n",
      "               'training on contrastive and spread out loss objectives. '\n",
      "               'Contrastive loss sharpens the embedding space, effectively '\n",
      "               'teaching notions of similarity and contrast. Positive pairs '\n",
      "               'pull closer together and negative pairs are pushed further '\n",
      "               'apart. We also use same tower negatives. Essentially, we add '\n",
      "               'more negative pairs between elements of the same modality, '\n",
      "               'pushing them further away from each other. This is crucial for '\n",
      "               'true omnimodal retrieval, so your results are not biased '\n",
      "               'towards any given modality. Finally, spread out loss ensures '\n",
      "               'the embeddings are dispersed to fully utilize the '\n",
      "               'expressiveness of the embedding space. This ensures the '\n",
      "               \"model's embeddings can be retrieved efficiently in vector \"\n",
      "               'databases and are robust to quantization. - The more '\n",
      "               'modalities you can embed, the more you can index, and storing '\n",
      "               'millions of vectors can quickly exhaust storage. Matryoshka '\n",
      "               'Representation Learning allows us to nest embeddings of '\n",
      "               'decreasing size. We apply loss functions to embedding prefixes '\n",
      "               'to ensure that even truncated versions of the embeddings are '\n",
      "               'powerful and complete representations. You can choose your '\n",
      "               'quality performance trade off without having to retrain or '\n",
      "               'change models. - With a unified model, you can run multimodal '\n",
      "               'retrieval, like searching a video library using the audio clip '\n",
      "               'or retrieving audio effects using text prompts in just a few '\n",
      "               'lines of Python. And with quantization aware training to '\n",
      "               \"compress the model's footprint down to INT4, EmbeddingGemma 2 \"\n",
      "               'runs even faster on edge devices. - EmbeddingGemma 2 is '\n",
      "               'smaller and faster, and now it sees, hears, and reads. '\n",
      "               'Download the model on Hugging Face and Kaggle or check out our '\n",
      "               \"cookbooks to get started. - We can't wait to see what you \"\n",
      "               'build with it. For more modeling details, read our technical '\n",
      "               'report in the link below.'}\n"
     ]
